WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.095
After a Go service loads a secret, the next question is where that value goes.

2
00:00:05.095 --> 00:00:19.267
Runtime consumers include database connection pools, HTTP clients that set Authorization headers, TLS clients that use private keys, webhook verifiers, token signers, message queue clients, and cloud SDKs.

3
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:24.681
A common mistake is to pass an entire config object everywhere because it is easy.

4
00:00:24.681 --> 00:00:33.758
That expands the number of packages, tests, logs, and developers that can accidentally touch the secret. Prefer narrow construction.

5
00:00:33.758 --> 00:00:39.729
The database package receives the database DSN or credential object because it needs it.

6
00:00:39.729 --> 00:00:45.382
The webhook verifier receives the webhook secret because it validates signatures.

7
00:00:45.382 --> 00:00:52.945
The HTTP client constructor receives a token source or credential provider because it attaches authorization.

8
00:00:52.945 --> 00:01:02.420
Other packages receive nonsecret settings or already constructed dependencies. This style makes the permission boundary visible in code.

9
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:09.904
Runtime memory deserves honest language. Go strings are convenient, immutable, and widely used by libraries.

10
00:01:09.904 --> 00:01:22.404
They are not ideal when you need guaranteed memory wiping, because copying, garbage collection, compiler behavior, and library internals make strong zeroization promises difficult.

11
00:01:22.404 --> 00:01:38.088
For most web services, practical controls usually matter more: use least privilege, prefer short-lived credentials, isolate processes, avoid unnecessary copies, avoid debug dumps, and keep secrets out of broad global variables.

12
00:01:38.088 --> 00:01:45.970
Database DSNs are a good example. A DSN may include host, username, password, database name, and connection options.

13
00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:55.922
It is tempting to log the DSN when connection setup fails. Do not. Log the safe parts: database alias, host if approved, environment, and error class.

14
00:01:55.922 --> 00:01:59.346
Pass the DSN only to the driver or connection layer.

15
00:01:59.346 --> 00:02:09.537
If your driver supports structured credential options or token sources, consider those patterns because they can make rotation and redaction easier.

16
00:02:09.537 --> 00:02:17.180
HTTP Authorization headers need similar treatment. Build them as close as possible to the outbound request path.

17
00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:26.973
Do not store full headers in shared request logs. If you must record request metadata, record that authorization was present, not the credential.

18
00:02:26.973 --> 00:02:37.323
TLS private keys and signing keys should be loaded through controlled paths and handed only to the TLS configuration or signing component that needs them.

19
00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:46.957
The runtime goal is controlled usefulness. A secret exists so the service can do required work. It should not become general application state.

20
00:02:46.957 --> 00:02:57.626
Keep it scoped, pass it deliberately, avoid exposing it through diagnostics, and assume that someday it will need to change while the service keeps operating.

21
00:02:57.626 --> 00:03:02.004
Be especially careful with helper functions that seem harmless.

22
00:03:02.004 --> 00:03:09.568
A function that builds an HTTP client, formats a DSN, or constructs a signer may create extra copies of the secret.

23
00:03:09.568 --> 00:03:13.708
That is sometimes unavoidable, but it should be intentional.

24
00:03:13.708 --> 00:03:25.412
Keep constructors narrow, avoid returning secret-bearing structs to broad callers, and document which component owns refresh or replacement when the credential changes.
